Quick test of `/dev/fd/*` to check for the possibility that it's bust on OpenBSD:

```
wilfred:/tmp> cat fd.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(int argc, char **argv)
{
        int fd, fd2, rv;
        char *p;
        char buf[16];
        ssize_t r;

        fd = open("/tmp/a", O_RDONLY);
        printf("fd=%d\n", fd);

        rv = asprintf(&p, "/dev/fd/%d", fd);
        printf("path=%s\n", p);

        fd2 = open(p, O_RDONLY);
        printf("fd2=%d\n", fd2);

        r = read(fd2, buf, 1);
        printf("read %zd bytes\n", r);
        printf("%c\n", buf[0]);

        return (EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
wilfred:/tmp> cat a
zzz
wilfred:/tmp> ./a.out 
fd=3
path=/dev/fd/3
fd2=4
read 1 bytes
z
```

Seems ok to me.

As for your above test:
```
wilfred:edd> stat -L <(ls)
1040 1820047 crw-rw-rw- 1 root wheel 5643 0 "Nov  7 17:30:38 2017" "Nov  7 17:30:38 2017" "Nov  7 17:30:38 2017" 65536 0 0 /dev/fd/11
```

I suppose the `c` in the mode means "character device".

Vim's own test? You mean regular vim?

@jamessan
